<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<IsTestProject>false</IsTestProject>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<!-- This project's public surface is largely undocumented, and TreatWarningsAsErrors would turn
		     every missing comment into a build failure. Removing this line is the forcing function for
		     the documentation pass; it is a bridge, not a decision. -->
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
		<Title>TestFramework.Container.Azure</Title>
		<Version>0.1.7</Version>
		<Authors>DeadMoon0</Authors>
		<Product>TestFramework</Product>
		<Description>Docker-backed environment providers for TestFramework timelines for Azure components.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/DeadMoon0/TestFramework-Container</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageIcon>Icon.png</PackageIcon>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/DeadMoon0/TestFramework-Container.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Test;UnitTest;IntegrationTest;Framework;Timeline;Docker;Azure</PackageTags>
		<PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
		<Copyright>Copyright (c) DeadMoon0 2026</Copyright>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Multi-target net8.0 and net10.0, and build on the shared TestFramework.Container blocks.
BREAKING: DockerAzureHostedCollectionFixture no longer implements xunit's IAsyncLifetime, so add the interface to your own fixture - the InitializeAsync/DisposeAsync it already has satisfy it. This is what removes xunit as a runtime dependency of this package, and it is what lets an xunit v3 consumer adapt the fixture at all; previously the base class hard-bound v2.
Faster: a Function App run now starts only the emulators its declared resource bindings need. It used to start Azurite, Cosmos, SQL Server and Service Bus every time, so a storage-only Function App paid for the Cosmos emulator - by far the slowest container in the stack - for nothing.
Isolation: a hosted environment reuses its containers across runs, which meant one run could see the blobs, tables, items and messages the previous run left behind. Declared resources are now purged between runs; set DockerAzureEnvironment.UseResetMode(AzureResetMode.None) to keep the old behaviour. A hosted collection must still disable test parallelization, or one run purges another's data.
Security: published ports bind to 127.0.0.1 rather than every interface, and the SQL Server password is generated per environment instead of being the constant shipped inside this package. That constant is now obsolete but still present, because a const is baked into consumers at compile time.
Also: a Function App's payload source can be declared with ContainerSource instead of inferred from a marker type, and the inference that remains no longer scans toward the drive root or fails on a directory it cannot read. Where it still infers, it now prefers the project's own output over the loaded one.</PackageReleaseNotes>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Runtime.CompilerServices.InternalsVisibleTo">
			<_Parameter1>TestFramework.Container.Azure.Tests</_Parameter1>
		</AssemblyAttribute>
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- Container is consumed as a published package, not as a project, so the dependency this
		     package declares is exactly the one a consumer resolves. Container must be published
		     before this package ships against it. -->
		<PackageReference Include="TestFramework.Container" Version="0.1.0" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="..\Modules\TestFramework-Common\Assets\Icon.png">
			<Pack>True</Pack>
			<PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
		</None>
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Azure.Messaging.ServiceBus" Version="7.20.1" />
		<!-- Tables and queues arrive transitively through TestFramework.Azure, but the per-run reset
		     compiles against both, so they are named here rather than depended on by accident. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Azure.Data.Tables" Version="12.9.1" />
		<PackageReference Include="Azure.Storage.Blobs" Version="12.26.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Azure.Storage.Queues" Version="12.26.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Azure.Cosmos" Version="3.59.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Data.SqlClient" Version="6.1.2" />
		<PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" />
		<!-- Direct reference overrides the vulnerable SSH.NET 2025.1.0 that Testcontainers pulls in
		     transitively (GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284). Testcontainers still pins it as of 4.13.0. -->
		<PackageReference Include="SSH.NET" Version="2026.0.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Testcontainers" Version="4.11.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.Azurite" Version="4.11.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.CosmosDb" Version="4.11.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.MsSql" Version="4.11.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.ServiceBus" Version="4.11.0" />
		<PackageReference Include="TestFramework.Azure" Version="0.1.13" />
		<PackageReference Include="TestFramework.Config" Version="0.1.6" />
		<PackageReference Include="TestFramework.Core" Version="0.1.10" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="README.md">
			<Pack>True</Pack>
			<PackagePath>\</PackagePath>
		</None>
		<None Update="Configurations\ServiceBus\config.json">
			<CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
		</None>
		<None Update="example.local.testsettings.json">
			<CopyToOutputDirectory>PreserveNewest</CopyToOutputDirectory>
		</None>
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>